(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("baremetal",{"baremetal/servers-racks-and-remote-management":{"html":"\u003cp>A virtual machine is a slice of someone else&#39;s server. On bare metal you own the whole machine, and you also own everything that surrounds it: the rack, the power, the cooling and the cable to the switch. This lesson shows the parts and the one tool that lets you manage them without being in the room.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The parts of a rack server and how racks are laid out\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Power, cooling and why a rack has a limit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The BMC: the small computer that manages the big one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>IPMI and Redfish, and the commands you use every week\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-is-in-the-box\">What is in the box\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A rack server is measured in \u003cstrong>rack units\u003c/strong>: 1U is 4.45 cm tall. Most fleets use 1U or 2U servers. Inside you find:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>CPUs\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>DIMMs\u003c/strong> (memory sticks), usually in pairs per channel\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Drives\u003c/strong>: SATA, SAS or NVMe, in front bays or inside\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Network cards\u003c/strong> (NICs), often two or four ports\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Power supplies\u003c/strong>, normally two, each fed from a different power line\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Fans\u003c/strong>, which pull air from the front (cold aisle) to the back (hot aisle)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>BMC\u003c/strong>, the baseboard management controller, a tiny separate computer on the motherboard\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"power-and-cooling-set-the-limit\">Power and cooling set the limit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A rack is not full when it runs out of space. It is full when it runs out of \u003cstrong>power\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>cooling\u003c/strong>. A rack with a 7 kW feed cannot hold twenty servers that each draw 500 W, even though they fit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Two power supplies on two separate feeds (A and B) mean one feed can fail and the server stays up, \u003cstrong>but only if each feed alone can carry the whole rack\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cold air enters at the front. Blanking panels in empty slots stop hot air from looping back round.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A hot spot at the top of a rack is usually a gap in the panels or a failed fan.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-bmc-a-computer-inside-the-computer\">The BMC: a computer inside the computer\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The BMC has its own network port, its own address and its own power, so it works when the main server is off or hung. You talk to it with \u003cstrong>IPMI\u003c/strong> (older, widely supported) or \u003cstrong>Redfish\u003c/strong> (newer, a REST API with JSON). Vendors brand it as iDRAC, iLO or IPMI/BMC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>With it you can power the server on or off, read the hardware event log, check temperatures and open a remote console, all without going near the machine.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ipmitool -H 10.20.0.50 -U admin -P secret chassis power status\nipmitool -H 10.20.0.50 -U admin -P secret sel list\nipmitool -H 10.20.0.50 -U admin -P secret chassis power on\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ipmitool -H 10.20.0.50 -U admin -P secret chassis power status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Chassis Power is off\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ipmitool -H 10.20.0.50 -U admin -P secret sel list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 1   | 11/02/2026 | 03:14:22 | Power Unit #0x01 | Power off/down | Asserted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 2   | 11/02/2026 | 03:14:25 | Temperature #0x31 | Upper Critical going high | Asserted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ipmitool -H 10.20.0.50 -U admin -P secret chassis power on\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Chassis Power Control: Up/On\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>system event log\u003c/strong> (SEL) is the first place to look. Here the server shut itself down because it overheated, which points at cooling and not at software.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-the-bmc-safe\">Keep the BMC safe\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The BMC can power-cycle a server and open a console, so treat its network as a management network: separate from production, not reachable from the internet, with its default password changed on day one.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> A server in a rack is hung and not answering SSH. You are in another city.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># ask someone on site to press the power button\n# or just keep trying to SSH\nssh ops@db-12\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>ssh: connect to host db-12 port 22: Connection timed out\n$ ping -c1 db-12\n1 packets transmitted, 0 received, 100% packet loss\n# two hours later, still no news from site\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> SSH needs the operating system, and the operating system is the thing that has hung. Waiting for a person to travel turns a ten-minute fix into hours. The BMC runs on its own and answers even when the host is dead.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>ipmitool -H 10.20.0.52 -U admin -P &quot;$BMC_PASS&quot; chassis power status\nipmitool -H 10.20.0.52 -U admin -P &quot;$BMC_PASS&quot; sel list | tail -5\nipmitool -H 10.20.0.52 -U admin -P &quot;$BMC_PASS&quot; chassis power cycle\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Read the event log first so you know why it hung, then power cycle through the BMC. Raise a ticket for the cause, not just the reboot.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-rack-that-tripped-at-noon\">Incident: The rack that tripped at noon\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Four servers in one rack went off within a minute at 12:04.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ipmitool -H 10.20.0.61 sel list | tail -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ipmitool -H 10.20.0.61 sel list | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 9   | 11/02/2026 | 12:04:11 | Power Supply #0x62 | Power Supply AC lost | Asserted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">10   | 11/02/2026 | 12:04:12 | Power Unit #0x01 | Redundancy Lost | Asserted\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>One power feed failed, as the design allows. But the extra servers added over the year had pushed the load so that feed B alone could not carry the rack. When A dropped, B tripped too. The fix was to add up the load on one feed and to stop adding servers to a rack once one feed cannot carry it alone.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Filling a rack by space and ignoring power and cooling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving the BMC on the production network with the default password\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Plugging both power supplies into the same feed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Having no way in but SSH, so a hung host needs a trip to the datacentre\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-is-in-the-box","text":"What is in the box"},{"id":"power-and-cooling-set-the-limit","text":"Power and cooling set the limit"},{"id":"the-bmc-a-computer-inside-the-computer","text":"The BMC: a computer inside the computer"},{"id":"keep-the-bmc-safe","text":"Keep the BMC safe"},{"id":"incident-the-rack-that-tripped-at-noon","text":"Incident: The rack that tripped at noon"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Redfish standard (DMTF)","u":"https://www.dmtf.org/standards/redfish"},{"t":"Open Compute Project","u":"https://www.opencomputeproject.org/"},{"t":"ipmitool project","u":"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/products/docs/servers/ipmi/ipmi-second-gen-interface-spec-v2-rev1-1.html"}],"text":"A virtual machine is a slice of someone else&#39;s server. On bare metal you own the whole machine, and you also own everything that surrounds it: the rack, the power, the cooling and the cable to the switch. This lesson shows the parts and the one tool that lets you manage them without being in the room. What you will learn The parts of a rack server and how racks are laid out Power, cooling and why a rack has a limit The BMC: the small computer that manages the big one IPMI and Redfish, and the commands you use every week What is in the box A rack server is measured in rack units : 1U is 4.45 cm tall. Most fleets use 1U or 2U servers. Inside you find: CPUs and DIMMs (memory sticks), usually in pairs per channel Drives : SATA, SAS or NVMe, in front bays or inside Network cards (NICs), often two or four ports Power supplies , normally two, each fed from a different power line Fans , which pull air from the front (cold aisle) to the back (hot aisle) A BMC , the baseboard management controller, a tiny separate computer on the motherboard Power and cooling set the limit A rack is not full when it runs out of space. It is full when it runs out of power or cooling . A rack with a 7 kW feed cannot hold twenty servers that each draw 500 W, even though they fit. Two power supplies on two separate feeds (A and B) mean one feed can fail and the server stays up, but only if each feed alone can carry the whole rack . Cold air enters at the front. Blanking panels in empty slots stop hot air from looping back round. A hot spot at the top of a rack is usually a gap in the panels or a failed fan. The BMC: a computer inside the computer The BMC has its own network port, its own address and its own power, so it works when the main server is off or hung. You talk to it with IPMI (older, widely supported) or Redfish (newer, a REST API with JSON). Vendors brand it as iDRAC, iLO or IPMI/BMC. With it you can power the server on or off, read the hardware event log, check temperatures and open a remote console, all without going near the machine. $ ipmitool -H 10.20.0.50 -U admin -P secret chassis power status Chassis Power is off $ ipmitool -H 10.20.0.50 -U admin -P secret sel list 1 | 11/02/2026 | 03:14:22 | Power Unit #0x01 | Power off/down | Asserted 2 | 11/02/2026 | 03:14:25 | Temperature #0x31 | Upper Critical going high | Asserted $ ipmitool -H 10.20.0.50 -U admin -P secret chassis power on Chassis Power Control: Up/On The system event log (SEL) is the first place to look. Here the server shut itself down because it overheated, which points at cooling and not at software. Keep the BMC safe The BMC can power-cycle a server and open a console, so treat its network as a management network: separate from production, not reachable from the internet, with its default password changed on day one. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. A server in a rack is hung and not answering SSH. You are in another city. The tempting way # ask someone on site to press the power button # or just keep trying to SSH ssh ops@db-12 See what goes wrong ssh: connect to host db-12 port 22: Connection timed out $ ping -c1 db-12 1 packets transmitted, 0 received, 100% packet loss # two hours later, still no news from site Why it fails. SSH needs the operating system, and the operating system is the thing that has hung. Waiting for a person to travel turns a ten-minute fix into hours. The BMC runs on its own and answers even when the host is dead. See the way that works The way that works ipmitool -H 10.20.0.52 -U admin -P \"$BMC_PASS\" chassis power status ipmitool -H 10.20.0.52 -U admin -P \"$BMC_PASS\" sel list | tail -5 ipmitool -H 10.20.0.52 -U admin -P \"$BMC_PASS\" chassis power cycle Read the event log first so you know why it hung, then power cycle through the BMC. Raise a ticket for the cause, not just the reboot. Incident: The rack that tripped at noon Incident Four servers in one rack went off within a minute at 12:04. $ ipmitool -H 10.20.0.61 sel list | tail -2 9"},"baremetal/provisioning-pxe-dhcp-and-images":{"html":"\u003cp>A new server arrives with empty disks. Nobody wants to install an operating system on a hundred machines by hand. Provisioning means the machine installs itself over the network, the same way every time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The boot chain: DHCP, TFTP or HTTP, a boot loader, an installer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What a kickstart, preseed or cloud-init file does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Image based provisioning compared with installing from packages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tools that manage the process: MAAS, Ironic and Foreman\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-boot-chain\">The boot chain\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>The server powers on and its network card sends a \u003cstrong>DHCP\u003c/strong> request. The BIOS or UEFI was set to try the network first.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The DHCP server answers with an IP address, plus two extra pieces of information: \u003ccode>next-server\u003c/code> (where the boot files are) and \u003ccode>filename\u003c/code> (which file to load).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The server fetches the boot loader (for example \u003ccode>pxelinux.0\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>grubx64.efi\u003c/code>) over \u003cstrong>TFTP\u003c/strong>. Modern setups chain to \u003cstrong>iPXE\u003c/strong>, which can use HTTP and is much faster.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The boot loader loads a kernel and an initial ramdisk and starts an installer.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The installer reads an \u003cstrong>answer file\u003c/strong> and installs without asking questions, then reboots from disk.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo journalctl -u dnsmasq -n 4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> journalctl -u dnsmasq -n 4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">dnsmasq-dhcp: DHCPDISCOVER(eth0) 3c:ec:ef:12:34:56\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">dnsmasq-dhcp: DHCPOFFER(eth0) 10.20.1.77 3c:ec:ef:12:34:56\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">dnsmasq-tftp: sent /srv/tftp/grubx64.efi to 10.20.1.77\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">dnsmasq-tftp: file /srv/tftp/grub.cfg-3c:ec:ef:12:34:56 not found\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The last line is normal: the loader first looks for a config file named after its own MAC address, then falls back to a default.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-answer-file\">The answer file\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Kickstart\u003c/strong> (Red Hat family), \u003cstrong>preseed\u003c/strong> (Debian) and \u003cstrong>autoinstall\u003c/strong> (Ubuntu) describe disks, packages, users and network.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>cloud-init\u003c/strong> runs on first boot and sets the hostname, keys and extra setup.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>A good rule: the installer makes the machine generic, and configuration management (Ansible, Puppet) makes it specific. That way, rebuilding is safe and repeatable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"images-instead-of-installs\">Images instead of installs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Installing packages on every machine is slow and each one drifts. \u003cstrong>Image based\u003c/strong> provisioning writes a prepared disk image to the machine, then lets cloud-init personalise it. It is faster and every node starts identical. OpenStack Ironic and MAAS both work this way.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tools\">Tools\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>MAAS\u003c/strong> (Canonical) discovers machines, controls power through the BMC and deploys images.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>OpenStack Ironic\u003c/strong> makes bare metal servers look like cloud instances.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Foreman\u003c/strong> with Katello manages Red Hat style installs and patching.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>All of them rest on the same chain, so when one fails you debug DHCP, then TFTP or HTTP, then the loader, then the installer.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> A new server PXE boots but never reaches the installer. It shows &quot;No bootable device&quot;.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># reinstall the operating system from a USB stick in the datacentre\n# and skip finding out why PXE failed\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># works once, but the next ten servers fail the same way\n# the DHCP config still has the wrong next-server\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Installing by hand hides the fault. The cause is in the DHCP answer, which every other new server also receives. Fixing one machine leaves the process broken.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>sudo journalctl -u dnsmasq -n 20\ngrep -E &quot;next-server|filename|dhcp-boot&quot; /etc/dnsmasq.conf\nsudo systemctl restart dnsmasq\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Watch the DHCP log while the server boots. If there is an offer but no TFTP transfer, the next-server address or the file name is wrong.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-every-new-node-booted-into-a-reinstall\">Incident: Every new node booted into a reinstall\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After an upgrade, nodes that were already in service rebooted into the installer and wiped themselves.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The boot order on those servers was network first. Normally the PXE server answered only unknown machines. After the change, it answered every machine, so a routine reboot ran the installer again. The fix was to answer only machines marked &quot;to be built&quot;, and to set the disk first in the boot order once a machine is deployed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Leaving network boot first on machines in service\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting settings inside the install image instead of in cloud-init or configuration management\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Having no DHCP log to read when PXE fails\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running a single provisioning server that is itself unavailable when you need it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-boot-chain","text":"The boot chain"},{"id":"the-answer-file","text":"The answer file"},{"id":"images-instead-of-installs","text":"Images instead of installs"},{"id":"tools","text":"Tools"},{"id":"incident-every-new-node-booted-into-a-reinstall","text":"Incident: Every new node booted into a reinstall"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Ubuntu autoinstall reference","u":"https://canonical-subiquity.readthedocs.io/en/latest/intro-to-autoinstall.html"},{"t":"iPXE documentation","u":"https://ipxe.org/docs"},{"t":"OpenStack Ironic documentation","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/ironic/latest/"}],"text":"A new server arrives with empty disks. Nobody wants to install an operating system on a hundred machines by hand. Provisioning means the machine installs itself over the network, the same way every time. What you will learn The boot chain: DHCP, TFTP or HTTP, a boot loader, an installer What a kickstart, preseed or cloud-init file does Image based provisioning compared with installing from packages Tools that manage the process: MAAS, Ironic and Foreman The boot chain The server powers on and its network card sends a DHCP request. The BIOS or UEFI was set to try the network first. The DHCP server answers with an IP address, plus two extra pieces of information: next-server (where the boot files are) and filename (which file to load). The server fetches the boot loader (for example pxelinux.0 or grubx64.efi ) over TFTP . Modern setups chain to iPXE , which can use HTTP and is much faster. The boot loader loads a kernel and an initial ramdisk and starts an installer. The installer reads an answer file and installs without asking questions, then reboots from disk. $ sudo journalctl -u dnsmasq -n 4 dnsmasq-dhcp: DHCPDISCOVER(eth0) 3c:ec:ef:12:34:56 dnsmasq-dhcp: DHCPOFFER(eth0) 10.20.1.77 3c:ec:ef:12:34:56 dnsmasq-tftp: sent /srv/tftp/grubx64.efi to 10.20.1.77 dnsmasq-tftp: file /srv/tftp/grub.cfg-3c:ec:ef:12:34:56 not found The last line is normal: the loader first looks for a config file named after its own MAC address, then falls back to a default. The answer file Kickstart (Red Hat family), preseed (Debian) and autoinstall (Ubuntu) describe disks, packages, users and network. cloud-init runs on first boot and sets the hostname, keys and extra setup. A good rule: the installer makes the machine generic, and configuration management (Ansible, Puppet) makes it specific. That way, rebuilding is safe and repeatable. Images instead of installs Installing packages on every machine is slow and each one drifts. Image based provisioning writes a prepared disk image to the machine, then lets cloud-init personalise it. It is faster and every node starts identical. OpenStack Ironic and MAAS both work this way. Tools MAAS (Canonical) discovers machines, controls power through the BMC and deploys images. OpenStack Ironic makes bare metal servers look like cloud instances. Foreman with Katello manages Red Hat style installs and patching. All of them rest on the same chain, so when one fails you debug DHCP, then TFTP or HTTP, then the loader, then the installer. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. A new server PXE boots but never reaches the installer. It shows \"No bootable device\". The tempting way # reinstall the operating system from a USB stick in the datacentre # and skip finding out why PXE failed See what goes wrong # works once, but the next ten servers fail the same way # the DHCP config still has the wrong next-server Why it fails. Installing by hand hides the fault. The cause is in the DHCP answer, which every other new server also receives. Fixing one machine leaves the process broken. See the way that works The way that works sudo journalctl -u dnsmasq -n 20 grep -E \"next-server|filename|dhcp-boot\" /etc/dnsmasq.conf sudo systemctl restart dnsmasq Watch the DHCP log while the server boots. If there is an offer but no TFTP transfer, the next-server address or the file name is wrong. Incident: Every new node booted into a reinstall Incident After an upgrade, nodes that were already in service rebooted into the installer and wiped themselves. The boot order on those servers was network first. Normally the PXE server answered only unknown machines. After the change, it answered every machine, so a routine reboot ran the installer again. The fix was to answer only machines marked \"to be built\", and to set the disk first in the boot order once a machine is deployed. Common mistakes Leaving network boot first on machines in service Putting settings inside the install image instead of in cloud-init or configuration management Havin"},"baremetal/disks-raid-firmware-and-burn-in":{"html":"\u003cp>Disks are the part of a server that wears out. On bare metal you choose them, protect them and replace them, so you need to know how they fail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>SATA, SAS and NVMe, and what each costs and delivers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>RAID levels and the difference between software and hardware RAID\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading SMART data and acting on it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Firmware and BIOS settings that matter\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Burn-in: testing a new machine before it carries load\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"drive-types\">Drive types\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>SATA\u003c/strong> is cheap and fine for capacity. \u003cstrong>SAS\u003c/strong> is built for servers: dual path, better error handling.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>NVMe\u003c/strong> connects directly over PCIe. It is far faster and handles many requests at once, which suits databases.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Drives are rated for \u003cstrong>endurance\u003c/strong> (how many times you can rewrite the whole disk per day). A write-heavy database on a read-optimised drive wears it out early.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"raid-in-short\">RAID in short\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What it does\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Survives\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stripes data for speed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Nothing\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Mirrors two disks\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One disk\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>5\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stripes with one parity\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One disk\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>6\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stripes with two parity\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Two disks\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>10\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Mirrored pairs, striped\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One per pair\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>RAID protects against a \u003cstrong>disk failing\u003c/strong>. It does not protect against deleting a file, a bad application update, or a fire. You still need backups.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Linux can do RAID in software with \u003ccode>mdadm\u003c/code>. Hardware RAID cards do it in firmware. Software RAID is easier to inspect and move between machines; hardware RAID often has a battery-backed write cache that helps write speed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo mdadm --detail /dev/md0 | grep -E &quot;State|Failed|Active&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> mdadm --detail /dev/md0 | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;State|Failed|Active&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">             State : clean, degraded\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    Active Devices : 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    Failed Devices : 1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Degraded\u003c/strong> means the array works but has lost its safety margin. Until the replacement finishes rebuilding, a second failure loses data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"smart-the-disk-s-own-report\">SMART: the disk&#39;s own report\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Disks monitor themselves. \u003ccode>smartctl\u003c/code> reads that report.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo smartctl -H /dev/sdb\nsudo smartctl -a /dev/sdb | grep -E &quot;Reallocated|Pending|Power_On&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> smartctl -H /dev/sdb\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">SMART overall-health self-assessment test result: FAILED!\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> smartctl -a /dev/sdb | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Reallocated|Pending|Power_On&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  5 Reallocated_Sector_Ct   098   098   010    120\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  9 Power_On_Hours          090   090   000    43000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">197 Current_Pending_Sector  100   100   000    8\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Rising \u003cstrong>reallocated\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>pending\u003c/strong> sectors mean the disk is running out of spare space. Replace it before it fails completely.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"firmware-and-bios\">Firmware and BIOS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Disks, NICs, the BMC and the BIOS all have firmware. Out-of-date firmware causes strange bugs and sometimes data loss. Update in a controlled order, one node at a time, and keep a record. Check BIOS settings such as boot mode (UEFI), power profile (performance, not power saving, for latency-sensitive work) and virtualisation support.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"burn-in\">Burn-in\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>New hardware fails early more often than old hardware. A \u003cstrong>burn-in\u003c/strong> run for a day or two catches it before real data lands there:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Memory test (\u003ccode>memtester\u003c/code> or the vendor tool)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>CPU stress (\u003ccode>stress-ng\u003c/code>)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disk write and read of the whole surface (\u003ccode>fio\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>badblocks\u003c/code>)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check the event log and SMART afterwards for any new errors\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> /dev/md0 is degraded after a disk failed. Restore redundancy.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># pull the failed disk out and put it back to see if it comes back\n# then wait without checking\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ mdadm --detail /dev/md0 | grep State\n             State : clean, degraded\n# a week later the second disk fails during a heavy job\nmdadm: /dev/md0 has failed, data is unavailable\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> A disk that was kicked out of an array is not trustworthy, and a reseat only hides the fault. The array stayed degraded for a week, so the second failure had nothing to protect against.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>sudo smartctl -H /dev/sdb\nsudo mdadm /dev/md0 --fail /dev/sdb\nsudo mdadm /dev/md0 --remove /dev/sdb\nsudo mdadm /dev/md0 --add /dev/sdc\nsudo mdadm --detail /dev/md0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Confirm the disk is really bad, replace it, and watch the rebuild finish. A degraded array is an incident until it shows clean.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-disks-one-batch\">Incident: Two disks, one batch\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A RAID 5 array lost a second disk during the rebuild after the first one failed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>All the disks came from the same batch, were installed on the same day and had the same hours on them. When the first failed, the rebuild put heavy load on the others, and the second failed within hours. After this the team used RAID 6 for large arrays, mixed batches, and made rebuild time part of their capacity plan.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating RAID as a backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring a degraded array for days\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping burn-in and finding bad memory in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Updating firmware on every node at once\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"drive-types","text":"Drive types"},{"id":"raid-in-short","text":"RAID in short"},{"id":"smart-the-disk-s-own-report","text":"SMART: the disk's own report"},{"id":"firmware-and-bios","text":"Firmware and BIOS"},{"id":"burn-in","text":"Burn-in"},{"id":"incident-two-disks-one-batch","text":"Incident: Two disks, one batch"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Linux RAID wiki (kernel.org)","u":"https://raid.wiki.kernel.org/"},{"t":"smartmontools documentation","u":"https://www.smartmontools.org/wiki/TocDoc"},{"t":"NVMe specifications","u":"https://nvmexpress.org/specifications/"}],"text":"Disks are the part of a server that wears out. On bare metal you choose them, protect them and replace them, so you need to know how they fail. What you will learn SATA, SAS and NVMe, and what each costs and delivers RAID levels and the difference between software and hardware RAID Reading SMART data and acting on it Firmware and BIOS settings that matter Burn-in: testing a new machine before it carries load Drive types SATA is cheap and fine for capacity. SAS is built for servers: dual path, better error handling. NVMe connects directly over PCIe. It is far faster and handles many requests at once, which suits databases. Drives are rated for endurance (how many times you can rewrite the whole disk per day). A write-heavy database on a read-optimised drive wears it out early. RAID in short Level What it does Survives 0 Stripes data for speed Nothing 1 Mirrors two disks One disk 5 Stripes with one parity One disk 6 Stripes with two parity Two disks 10 Mirrored pairs, striped One per pair RAID protects against a disk failing . It does not protect against deleting a file, a bad application update, or a fire. You still need backups. Linux can do RAID in software with mdadm . Hardware RAID cards do it in firmware. Software RAID is easier to inspect and move between machines; hardware RAID often has a battery-backed write cache that helps write speed. $ sudo mdadm --detail /dev/md0 | grep -E \"State|Failed|Active\" State : clean, degraded Active Devices : 1 Failed Devices : 1 Degraded means the array works but has lost its safety margin. Until the replacement finishes rebuilding, a second failure loses data. SMART: the disk&#39;s own report Disks monitor themselves. smartctl reads that report. $ sudo smartctl -H /dev/sdb SMART overall-health self-assessment test result: FAILED! $ sudo smartctl -a /dev/sdb | grep -E \"Reallocated|Pending|Power_On\" 5 Reallocated_Sector_Ct 098 098 010 120 9 Power_On_Hours 090 090 000 43000 197 Current_Pending_Sector 100 100 000 8 Rising reallocated or pending sectors mean the disk is running out of spare space. Replace it before it fails completely. Firmware and BIOS Disks, NICs, the BMC and the BIOS all have firmware. Out-of-date firmware causes strange bugs and sometimes data loss. Update in a controlled order, one node at a time, and keep a record. Check BIOS settings such as boot mode (UEFI), power profile (performance, not power saving, for latency-sensitive work) and virtualisation support. Burn-in New hardware fails early more often than old hardware. A burn-in run for a day or two catches it before real data lands there: Memory test ( memtester or the vendor tool) CPU stress ( stress-ng ) Disk write and read of the whole surface ( fio or badblocks ) Check the event log and SMART afterwards for any new errors Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. /dev/md0 is degraded after a disk failed. Restore redundancy. The tempting way # pull the failed disk out and put it back to see if it comes back # then wait without checking See what goes wrong $ mdadm --detail /dev/md0 | grep State State : clean, degraded # a week later the second disk fails during a heavy job mdadm: /dev/md0 has failed, data is unavailable Why it fails. A disk that was kicked out of an array is not trustworthy, and a reseat only hides the fault. The array stayed degraded for a week, so the second failure had nothing to protect against. See the way that works The way that works sudo smartctl -H /dev/sdb sudo mdadm /dev/md0 --fail /dev/sdb sudo mdadm /dev/md0 --remove /dev/sdb sudo mdadm /dev/md0 --add /dev/sdc sudo mdadm --detail /dev/md0 Confirm the disk is really bad, replace it, and watch the rebuild finish. A degraded array is an incident until it shows clean. Incident: Two disks, one batch Incident A RAID 5 array lost a second disk during the rebuild after the first one failed. All the disks came from the same batch, were installed on the same day and had the same hours on them. When the first failed, the rebuild put heavy loa"},"baremetal/datacenter-networking-leaf-spine-and-bonding":{"html":"\u003cp>A server is only useful when it can reach other servers. In a datacentre, the way racks connect decides how fast and how reliably they can talk.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Top-of-rack switches and the leaf-spine layout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why east-west traffic changed the design\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Bonding and LACP for redundant links\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>VLANs and the separate management network\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cabling mistakes that cause hard-to-find faults\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"top-of-rack-and-leaf-spine\">Top-of-rack and leaf-spine\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each rack has a \u003cstrong>top-of-rack (ToR) switch\u003c/strong>, which connects the servers in that rack. In a \u003cstrong>leaf-spine\u003c/strong> design the ToR switches are the \u003cstrong>leaves\u003c/strong>, and every leaf connects to every \u003cstrong>spine\u003c/strong> switch.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Any two servers are at most three switch hops apart (leaf, spine, leaf).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>More capacity means adding spines. More racks mean adding leaves.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Traffic is spread over many equal-cost paths with \u003cstrong>ECMP\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>This suits modern workloads, where most traffic is \u003cstrong>east-west\u003c/strong> (server to server inside the datacentre) and not north-south (in and out).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"bonding-and-lacp\">Bonding and LACP\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A server with two NIC ports can join them into one logical link, called a \u003cstrong>bond\u003c/strong> (Linux) or a team. This gives redundancy and, with the right mode, more bandwidth.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Mode\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What it does\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Switch support needed\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>active-backup\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One link used, the other waits\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>None\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>802.3ad (LACP)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Both links used, negotiated\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Switch must run LACP\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>balance-rr\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Packets spread round-robin\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Often causes reordering\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>LACP needs both sides to agree. If the server says 802.3ad but the switch ports are not in a port channel, the link will not form properly.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat /proc/net/bonding/bond0 | grep -E &quot;Mode|Slave Interface|MII Status&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> /proc/net/bonding/bond0 | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Mode|Slave Interface|MII Status&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Bonding Mode: IEEE 802.3ad Dynamic link aggregation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">MII Status: up\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Slave Interface: eno1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">MII Status: up\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Slave Interface: eno2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">MII Status: down\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>One slave is down: either the cable, the optic or the switch port is at fault.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"vlans-and-the-management-network\">VLANs and the management network\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>VLAN\u003c/strong> splits one physical network into separate logical ones. A common set: production, storage, a provisioning network for PXE, and a \u003cstrong>management\u003c/strong> network for the BMCs. Keep management traffic apart from production traffic so a problem or an attacker on one cannot reach the other.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cabling\">Cabling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Many faults are physical: a bent fibre, a dirty connector, a wrong optic, a cable plugged into the wrong port. Label both ends and keep a record of which server port goes to which switch port. Check counters with \u003ccode>ip -s link\u003c/code> for errors and drops.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Add bandwidth to a database server by joining its two NIC ports.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># set the bond to round-robin on the server only\necho &quot;BONDING_OPTS='mode=balance-rr miimon=100'&quot; &gt;&gt; /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-bond0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ iperf3 -c 10.20.2.10\n[  5]   0.00-10.00  sec  3.2 GBytes  2.75 Gbits/sec  41200 retr\n# many retransmits, and the throughput is worse than a single link\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Round-robin sends packets of one connection down both links. They arrive out of order, so TCP slows down and retransmits. The switch also does not know about the bond, so it learns the MAC on two ports and flaps.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># server side\nBONDING_OPTS='mode=802.3ad miimon=100 lacp_rate=fast xmit_hash_policy=layer3+4'\n# switch side: put both ports in one LACP port channel, same VLANs\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>With LACP both ends agree. Each flow stays on one link, and many flows spread across both.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-server-that-dropped-off-every-ten-minutes\">Incident: A server that dropped off every ten minutes\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A server lost network for a few seconds, over and over.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"dmesg | grep -i bond | tail -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>dmesg | grep -i bond | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">bond0: link status definitely down for interface eno2, disabling it\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">bond0: link status definitely up for interface eno2, 10000 Mbps full duplex\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cable was seated badly in one port, so the link bounced. The bond kept the server up, but the switch logged flapping. Replacing the cable fixed it. The lesson: bonding hides physical faults, so alert on link changes, not only on the server being down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Configuring LACP on only one side\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting management and production traffic on one network\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not labelling cables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hiding link flaps because the bond keeps traffic flowing\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"top-of-rack-and-leaf-spine","text":"Top-of-rack and leaf-spine"},{"id":"bonding-and-lacp","text":"Bonding and LACP"},{"id":"vlans-and-the-management-network","text":"VLANs and the management network"},{"id":"cabling","text":"Cabling"},{"id":"incident-a-server-that-dropped-off-every-ten-minutes","text":"Incident: A server that dropped off every ten minutes"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Linux kernel: bonding","u":"https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/networking/bonding.html"},{"t":"Open Compute Project networking","u":"https://www.opencomputeproject.org/"},{"t":"RFC 7938: BGP in large datacenters","u":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7938"}],"text":"A server is only useful when it can reach other servers. In a datacentre, the way racks connect decides how fast and how reliably they can talk. What you will learn Top-of-rack switches and the leaf-spine layout Why east-west traffic changed the design Bonding and LACP for redundant links VLANs and the separate management network Cabling mistakes that cause hard-to-find faults Top-of-rack and leaf-spine Each rack has a top-of-rack (ToR) switch , which connects the servers in that rack. In a leaf-spine design the ToR switches are the leaves , and every leaf connects to every spine switch. Any two servers are at most three switch hops apart (leaf, spine, leaf). More capacity means adding spines. More racks mean adding leaves. Traffic is spread over many equal-cost paths with ECMP . This suits modern workloads, where most traffic is east-west (server to server inside the datacentre) and not north-south (in and out). Bonding and LACP A server with two NIC ports can join them into one logical link, called a bond (Linux) or a team. This gives redundancy and, with the right mode, more bandwidth. Mode What it does Switch support needed active-backup One link used, the other waits None 802.3ad (LACP) Both links used, negotiated Switch must run LACP balance-rr Packets spread round-robin Often causes reordering LACP needs both sides to agree. If the server says 802.3ad but the switch ports are not in a port channel, the link will not form properly. $ cat /proc/net/bonding/bond0 | grep -E \"Mode|Slave Interface|MII Status\" Bonding Mode: IEEE 802.3ad Dynamic link aggregation MII Status: up Slave Interface: eno1 MII Status: up Slave Interface: eno2 MII Status: down One slave is down: either the cable, the optic or the switch port is at fault. VLANs and the management network A VLAN splits one physical network into separate logical ones. A common set: production, storage, a provisioning network for PXE, and a management network for the BMCs. Keep management traffic apart from production traffic so a problem or an attacker on one cannot reach the other. Cabling Many faults are physical: a bent fibre, a dirty connector, a wrong optic, a cable plugged into the wrong port. Label both ends and keep a record of which server port goes to which switch port. Check counters with ip -s link for errors and drops. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Add bandwidth to a database server by joining its two NIC ports. The tempting way # set the bond to round-robin on the server only echo \"BONDING_OPTS='mode=balance-rr miimon=100'\" >> /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-bond0 See what goes wrong $ iperf3 -c 10.20.2.10 [ 5] 0.00-10.00 sec 3.2 GBytes 2.75 Gbits/sec 41200 retr # many retransmits, and the throughput is worse than a single link Why it fails. Round-robin sends packets of one connection down both links. They arrive out of order, so TCP slows down and retransmits. The switch also does not know about the bond, so it learns the MAC on two ports and flaps. See the way that works The way that works # server side BONDING_OPTS='mode=802.3ad miimon=100 lacp_rate=fast xmit_hash_policy=layer3+4' # switch side: put both ports in one LACP port channel, same VLANs With LACP both ends agree. Each flow stays on one link, and many flows spread across both. Incident: A server that dropped off every ten minutes Incident A server lost network for a few seconds, over and over. $ dmesg | grep -i bond | tail -3 bond0: link status definitely down for interface eno2, disabling it bond0: link status definitely up for interface eno2, 10000 Mbps full duplex The cable was seated badly in one port, so the link bounced. The bond kept the server up, but the switch logged flapping. Replacing the cable fixed it. The lesson: bonding hides physical faults, so alert on link changes, not only on the server being down. Common mistakes Configuring LACP on only one side Putting management and production traffic on one network Not labelling cables Hiding link flaps because the bond "},"baremetal/operating-a-fleet-failures-lifecycle-and-capacity":{"html":"\u003cp>With hundreds of servers, hardware failure is routine, not exceptional. Operating bare metal well means finding faults quickly, replacing parts safely and planning ahead.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading hardware events: ECC memory, disks, fans, power\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A repeatable method for a sick server\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The lifecycle: receive, rack, provision, run, repair, retire\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spares, warranty and capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How bare metal and the cloud differ in who fixes what\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-hardware-failures\">Common hardware failures\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Sign\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Likely cause\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>First check\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Random reboots\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Failing memory or power supply\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>BMC event log\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Correctable ECC errors\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A DIMM starting to fail\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Which slot, how often\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Slow disk, many retries\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Disk with bad sectors\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>smartctl -a\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Fan or temperature alarms\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Failed fan, blocked airflow\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ipmitool sensor list\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Network errors\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Bad cable, optic or port\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ip -s link\u003c/code>, switch counters\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>ECC memory\u003c/strong> corrects single-bit errors silently. A few correctable errors are normal. A DIMM that keeps reporting them is likely to produce an \u003cstrong>uncorrectable\u003c/strong> error soon, which crashes the machine. Replace it at the first maintenance window.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ipmitool sel elist | grep -i ecc\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ipmitool sel elist | grep -i ecc\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 3 | 11/02/2026 | 03:14:22 | Memory #0x87 | Correctable ECC | Asserted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 4 | 11/02/2026 | 03:41:09 | Memory #0x87 | Correctable ECC | Asserted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 5 | 11/02/2026 | 04:02:55 | Memory #0x87 | Correctable ECC | Asserted\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The same sensor three times in an hour points at one DIMM slot.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-method-for-a-sick-server\">A method for a sick server\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Drain\u003c/strong> it: move workloads away so repair is safe.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Read\u003c/strong> the evidence: BMC log, SMART, kernel log, switch counters.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Decide\u003c/strong> the part: do not swap parts at random.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Replace\u003c/strong> one thing, then test.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Record\u003c/strong> it, so a repeat failure on the same machine shows up.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Return it to service only after it passes a short burn-in.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lifecycle\">Lifecycle\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A server goes through receiving (check against the order and asset tag), racking, provisioning, service, repair and retirement. Retirement includes \u003cstrong>wiping disks securely\u003c/strong> and removing the machine from inventory, monitoring, DNS and the configuration database. Forgotten machines keep drawing power and keep old credentials alive.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"spares-and-warranty\">Spares and warranty\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keep spare drives, DIMMs, power supplies and cables on site, since shipping can take days.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Know the warranty terms. Vendors usually ship the part but you replace it, unless you buy on-site service.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Track \u003cstrong>mean time between failures\u003c/strong> across your fleet. If one batch fails faster, ask the vendor early.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity\">Capacity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Plan for losing a rack and a feed. Leave room so that when machines are out for repair, the rest still carry the load. Order lead times for hardware can be weeks or months, so plan quarters ahead, not days.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> A server crashes about once a week. Stop the crashes.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># reboot it each time and replace the disk &quot;just in case&quot;\nsudo reboot\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ ipmitool sel elist | grep -c ECC\n214\n# the disk change changes nothing, the crashes continue\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Rebooting clears the symptom and the disk was never the cause. The event log shows hundreds of correctable memory errors from one slot. The fault was a DIMM that grew worse until it caused an uncorrectable error.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>ipmitool sel elist | grep -i memory\n# note the slot, drain the host, replace that DIMM, run a memory test\nipmitool sel clear\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Use the evidence the hardware already collects. Replace the part it points to, and clear the log only after you have saved it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-fleet-wide-reboot-loop-after-a-firmware-update\">Incident: A fleet-wide reboot loop after a firmware update\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a firmware update across 120 servers, 30 would not come back.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The update was pushed to every host at once. A batch with an older BMC version needed an intermediate firmware first, and without it the BMC hung. With no BMC, the team had to visit the datacentre. Afterwards updates went in waves of five, starting with one canary, with a check that each host was healthy before the next wave.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Replacing parts at random\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Updating firmware everywhere at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to wipe disks and remove retired servers from inventory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Having no spares and waiting days for a cable\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"common-hardware-failures","text":"Common hardware failures"},{"id":"a-method-for-a-sick-server","text":"A method for a sick server"},{"id":"lifecycle","text":"Lifecycle"},{"id":"spares-and-warranty","text":"Spares and warranty"},{"id":"capacity","text":"Capacity"},{"id":"incident-a-fleet-wide-reboot-loop-after-a-firmware-update","text":"Incident: A fleet-wide reboot loop after a firmware update"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Open Compute Project hardware management","u":"https://www.opencomputeproject.org/"},{"t":"Redfish standard (DMTF)","u":"https://www.dmtf.org/standards/redfish"},{"t":"Google SRE book: Managing incidents","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-incidents/"}],"text":"With hundreds of servers, hardware failure is routine, not exceptional. Operating bare metal well means finding faults quickly, replacing parts safely and planning ahead. What you will learn Reading hardware events: ECC memory, disks, fans, power A repeatable method for a sick server The lifecycle: receive, rack, provision, run, repair, retire Spares, warranty and capacity How bare metal and the cloud differ in who fixes what Common hardware failures Sign Likely cause First check Random reboots Failing memory or power supply BMC event log Correctable ECC errors A DIMM starting to fail Which slot, how often Slow disk, many retries Disk with bad sectors smartctl -a Fan or temperature alarms Failed fan, blocked airflow ipmitool sensor list Network errors Bad cable, optic or port ip -s link , switch counters ECC memory corrects single-bit errors silently. A few correctable errors are normal. A DIMM that keeps reporting them is likely to produce an uncorrectable error soon, which crashes the machine. Replace it at the first maintenance window. $ ipmitool sel elist | grep -i ecc 3 | 11/02/2026 | 03:14:22 | Memory #0x87 | Correctable ECC | Asserted 4 | 11/02/2026 | 03:41:09 | Memory #0x87 | Correctable ECC | Asserted 5 | 11/02/2026 | 04:02:55 | Memory #0x87 | Correctable ECC | Asserted The same sensor three times in an hour points at one DIMM slot. A method for a sick server Drain it: move workloads away so repair is safe. Read the evidence: BMC log, SMART, kernel log, switch counters. Decide the part: do not swap parts at random. Replace one thing, then test. Record it, so a repeat failure on the same machine shows up. Return it to service only after it passes a short burn-in. Lifecycle A server goes through receiving (check against the order and asset tag), racking, provisioning, service, repair and retirement. Retirement includes wiping disks securely and removing the machine from inventory, monitoring, DNS and the configuration database. Forgotten machines keep drawing power and keep old credentials alive. Spares and warranty Keep spare drives, DIMMs, power supplies and cables on site, since shipping can take days. Know the warranty terms. Vendors usually ship the part but you replace it, unless you buy on-site service. Track mean time between failures across your fleet. If one batch fails faster, ask the vendor early. Capacity Plan for losing a rack and a feed. Leave room so that when machines are out for repair, the rest still carry the load. Order lead times for hardware can be weeks or months, so plan quarters ahead, not days. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. A server crashes about once a week. Stop the crashes. The tempting way # reboot it each time and replace the disk \"just in case\" sudo reboot See what goes wrong $ ipmitool sel elist | grep -c ECC 214 # the disk change changes nothing, the crashes continue Why it fails. Rebooting clears the symptom and the disk was never the cause. The event log shows hundreds of correctable memory errors from one slot. The fault was a DIMM that grew worse until it caused an uncorrectable error. See the way that works The way that works ipmitool sel elist | grep -i memory # note the slot, drain the host, replace that DIMM, run a memory test ipmitool sel clear Use the evidence the hardware already collects. Replace the part it points to, and clear the log only after you have saved it. Incident: A fleet-wide reboot loop after a firmware update Incident After a firmware update across 120 servers, 30 would not come back. The update was pushed to every host at once. A batch with an older BMC version needed an intermediate firmware first, and without it the BMC hung. With no BMC, the team had to visit the datacentre. Afterwards updates went in waves of five, starting with one canary, with a check that each host was healthy before the next wave. Common mistakes Replacing parts at random Updating firmware everywhere at once Forgetting to wipe disks and remove retired servers from inven"},"baremetal/bmc-ipmi-and-redfish-automation":{"html":"\u003cp>When a server hangs or has no operating system, you still need to reach it. The baseboard management controller, or BMC, is a separate chip with its own network port that gives you power control, console and hardware health without touching the host. Redfish is the modern, secure API on top of it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What the BMC is and why it works when the host is off\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>IPMI versus Redfish and their security differences\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scripting power, boot device and sensor reads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping the management network safe\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ipmi-and-redfish\">IPMI and Redfish\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>IPMI\u003c/strong> is the older protocol. It is widely supported but has weak authentication and many known issues, so use it only on a locked management network. \u003cstrong>Redfish\u003c/strong> is a REST and JSON API over HTTPS that covers inventory, power, boot, firmware and sensors with modern authentication.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# IPMI over the LAN\nipmitool -I lanplus -H 10.20.0.15 -U admin -P &quot;$BMC_PASS&quot; chassis power status\nipmitool -I lanplus -H 10.20.0.15 -U admin -P &quot;$BMC_PASS&quot; chassis bootdev pxe options=efiboot\n\n# Redfish: read power state and reset the system\ncurl -sk -u &quot;admin:$BMC_PASS&quot; https://10.20.0.15/redfish/v1/Systems/1 | jq '.PowerState,.Status'\ncurl -sk -u &quot;admin:$BMC_PASS&quot; -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{&quot;ResetType&quot;:&quot;GracefulRestart&quot;}' \\\n  https://10.20.0.15/redfish/v1/Systems/1/Actions/ComputerSystem.Reset\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># IPMI over the LAN\u003c/span>\nipmitool -I lanplus -H 10.20.0.15 -U admin -P \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$BMC_PASS\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> chassis power status\nipmitool -I lanplus -H 10.20.0.15 -U admin -P \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$BMC_PASS\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> chassis bootdev pxe options=efiboot\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Redfish: read power state and reset the system\u003c/span>\ncurl -sk -u \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;admin:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$BMC_PASS\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> https://10.20.0.15/redfish/v1/Systems/1 | jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.PowerState,.Status&#x27;\u003c/span>\ncurl -sk -u \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;admin:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$BMC_PASS\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -X POST -H \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Content-Type: application/json&#x27;\u003c/span> \\\n  -d \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;ResetType&quot;:&quot;GracefulRestart&quot;}&#x27;\u003c/span> \\\n  https://10.20.0.15/redfish/v1/Systems/1/Actions/ComputerSystem.Reset\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"automating-a-fleet\">Automating a fleet\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep an inventory that maps each server to its BMC address, and fetch credentials from a secrets manager instead of a file. Wrap actions in a small script that does one server at a time, logs who did what and has a dry-run mode. Common jobs are rebooting a hung node, setting one-time PXE boot before reinstall, reading temperature and fan sensors, and collecting the system event log when a node fails.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keeping-it-safe\">Keeping it safe\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Put every BMC on a separate \u003cstrong>management network\u003c/strong> with firewall rules that allow only a jump host. Change default accounts, disable unused services such as old IPMI cipher suites, update BMC firmware on a schedule and send BMC logs to your central logging system. A BMC compromise survives operating system reinstalls, so prevention matters.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-open-management-network\">Incident: The open management network\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Many BMCs were reachable on the office network with default logins.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"nmap -p 623,443 10.20.0.0/24 --open\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>nmap -p 623,443 10.20.0.0/24 --open\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">10.20.0.15  623/udp open  asf-rmcp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">10.20.0.15  login admin:admin accepted\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An audit found that BMCs were on the same network as laptops and still used factory credentials. Anyone on the network could power machines off or mount a virtual disk. The team moved all BMCs to an isolated VLAN behind a jump host, rotated unique passwords from a vault and added alerts for new BMC logins.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Leaving default BMC credentials in place\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting BMCs on the production network\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never updating BMC firmware\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scripts that reboot many servers at once without a limit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"ipmi-and-redfish","text":"IPMI and Redfish"},{"id":"automating-a-fleet","text":"Automating a fleet"},{"id":"keeping-it-safe","text":"Keeping it safe"},{"id":"incident-the-open-management-network","text":"Incident: The open management network"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"DMTF Redfish standard","u":"https://www.dmtf.org/standards/redfish"},{"t":"Redfish API specification","u":"https://redfish.dmtf.org/"}],"text":"When a server hangs or has no operating system, you still need to reach it. The baseboard management controller, or BMC, is a separate chip with its own network port that gives you power control, console and hardware health without touching the host. Redfish is the modern, secure API on top of it. What you will learn What the BMC is and why it works when the host is off IPMI versus Redfish and their security differences Scripting power, boot device and sensor reads Keeping the management network safe IPMI and Redfish IPMI is the older protocol. It is widely supported but has weak authentication and many known issues, so use it only on a locked management network. Redfish is a REST and JSON API over HTTPS that covers inventory, power, boot, firmware and sensors with modern authentication. # IPMI over the LAN ipmitool -I lanplus -H 10.20.0.15 -U admin -P \" $BMC_PASS \" chassis power status ipmitool -I lanplus -H 10.20.0.15 -U admin -P \" $BMC_PASS \" chassis bootdev pxe options=efiboot # Redfish: read power state and reset the system curl -sk -u \"admin: $BMC_PASS \" https://10.20.0.15/redfish/v1/Systems/1 | jq &#x27;.PowerState,.Status&#x27; curl -sk -u \"admin: $BMC_PASS \" -X POST -H &#x27;Content-Type: application/json&#x27; \\ -d &#x27;{\"ResetType\":\"GracefulRestart\"}&#x27; \\ https://10.20.0.15/redfish/v1/Systems/1/Actions/ComputerSystem.Reset Automating a fleet Keep an inventory that maps each server to its BMC address, and fetch credentials from a secrets manager instead of a file. Wrap actions in a small script that does one server at a time, logs who did what and has a dry-run mode. Common jobs are rebooting a hung node, setting one-time PXE boot before reinstall, reading temperature and fan sensors, and collecting the system event log when a node fails. Keeping it safe Put every BMC on a separate management network with firewall rules that allow only a jump host. Change default accounts, disable unused services such as old IPMI cipher suites, update BMC firmware on a schedule and send BMC logs to your central logging system. A BMC compromise survives operating system reinstalls, so prevention matters. Incident: The open management network Incident Many BMCs were reachable on the office network with default logins. $ nmap -p 623,443 10.20.0.0/24 --open 10.20.0.15 623/udp open asf-rmcp 10.20.0.15 login admin:admin accepted An audit found that BMCs were on the same network as laptops and still used factory credentials. Anyone on the network could power machines off or mount a virtual disk. The team moved all BMCs to an isolated VLAN behind a jump host, rotated unique passwords from a vault and added alerts for new BMC logins. Common mistakes Leaving default BMC credentials in place Putting BMCs on the production network Never updating BMC firmware Scripts that reboot many servers at once without a limit"},"baremetal/uefi-bootloaders-and-secure-boot":{"html":"\u003cp>A server is only as trustworthy as the first code it runs. Modern machines use UEFI firmware, an EFI system partition and a chain of signed bootloaders. Knowing this chain makes boot failures and security questions much less mysterious.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The UEFI boot sequence from firmware to kernel\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading and changing boot entries with efibootmgr\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How Secure Boot verifies shim, bootloader and kernel\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Recovering from a machine that will not boot\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-chain\">The chain\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Firmware reads \u003cstrong>boot entries\u003c/strong> from NVRAM and starts the first valid one. That loads a bootloader such as GRUB from the \u003cstrong>EFI system partition\u003c/strong> (ESP), a small FAT partition mounted at \u003ccode>/boot/efi\u003c/code>. The bootloader loads the kernel and initramfs, and the kernel mounts the root filesystem.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"efibootmgr -v\nmount | grep efi\nls /boot/efi/EFI\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">efibootmgr -v\nmount | grep efi\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /boot/efi/EFI\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">BootCurrent: 0003\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">BootOrder: 0003,0001,0000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Boot0003* ubuntu  HD(1,GPT,...)/File(\\EFI\\ubuntu\\shimx64.efi)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Boot0001* UEFI PXE IPv4 Intel(R) Ethernet\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Change the order with \u003ccode>efibootmgr -o 0003,0001\u003c/code> and set a one-time next boot with \u003ccode>efibootmgr -n 0001\u003c/code>. Always keep a working entry before removing another.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secure-boot\">Secure Boot\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With \u003cstrong>Secure Boot\u003c/strong>, firmware only runs code signed by a key it trusts. Distributions use a small signed program called \u003cstrong>shim\u003c/strong>, which verifies GRUB and the kernel against the vendor key or a Machine Owner Key you enroll. Unsigned custom kernels or out-of-tree modules fail unless you sign them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"mokutil --sb-state\nmokutil --list-enrolled | head\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">mokutil --sb-state\nmokutil --list-enrolled | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recovery\">Recovery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If a node will not boot, use the BMC console to see the error. Boot a rescue image, mount the root and ESP, then reinstall the bootloader and regenerate configuration. Keep a tested rescue image on your provisioning server so fixing a node is routine.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-kernel-that-would-not-start\">Incident: The kernel that would not start\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A custom kernel was rejected after Secure Boot was enabled.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: bad shim signature\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: you need to load the kernel first\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Press any key to continue\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A fleet moved to Secure Boot by policy, but the in-house kernel was unsigned. Nodes stopped at the loader. The team signed the kernel with an enrolled key, kept the signing key in a hardware module and added a boot test on one canary node before every rollout.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Updating firmware on the whole fleet at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Removing the old boot entry before testing the new one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disabling Secure Boot instead of signing correctly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not backing up the ESP contents\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-chain","text":"The chain"},{"id":"secure-boot","text":"Secure Boot"},{"id":"recovery","text":"Recovery"},{"id":"incident-the-kernel-that-would-not-start","text":"Incident: The kernel that would not start"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"UEFI specifications","u":"https://wiki.ubuntu.com/UEFI"},{"t":"Ubuntu Secure Boot","u":"https://wiki.ubuntu.com/UEFI/SecureBoot"}],"text":"A server is only as trustworthy as the first code it runs. Modern machines use UEFI firmware, an EFI system partition and a chain of signed bootloaders. Knowing this chain makes boot failures and security questions much less mysterious. What you will learn The UEFI boot sequence from firmware to kernel Reading and changing boot entries with efibootmgr How Secure Boot verifies shim, bootloader and kernel Recovering from a machine that will not boot The chain Firmware reads boot entries from NVRAM and starts the first valid one. That loads a bootloader such as GRUB from the EFI system partition (ESP), a small FAT partition mounted at /boot/efi . The bootloader loads the kernel and initramfs, and the kernel mounts the root filesystem. efibootmgr -v mount | grep efi ls /boot/efi/EFI BootCurrent: 0003 BootOrder: 0003,0001,0000 Boot0003* ubuntu HD(1,GPT,...)/File(\\EFI\\ubuntu\\shimx64.efi) Boot0001* UEFI PXE IPv4 Intel(R) Ethernet Change the order with efibootmgr -o 0003,0001 and set a one-time next boot with efibootmgr -n 0001 . Always keep a working entry before removing another. Secure Boot With Secure Boot , firmware only runs code signed by a key it trusts. Distributions use a small signed program called shim , which verifies GRUB and the kernel against the vendor key or a Machine Owner Key you enroll. Unsigned custom kernels or out-of-tree modules fail unless you sign them. mokutil --sb-state mokutil --list-enrolled | head Recovery If a node will not boot, use the BMC console to see the error. Boot a rescue image, mount the root and ESP, then reinstall the bootloader and regenerate configuration. Keep a tested rescue image on your provisioning server so fixing a node is routine. Incident: The kernel that would not start Incident A custom kernel was rejected after Secure Boot was enabled. error: bad shim signature error: you need to load the kernel first Press any key to continue A fleet moved to Secure Boot by policy, but the in-house kernel was unsigned. Nodes stopped at the loader. The team signed the kernel with an enrolled key, kept the signing key in a hardware module and added a boot test on one canary node before every rollout. Common mistakes Updating firmware on the whole fleet at once Removing the old boot entry before testing the new one Disabling Secure Boot instead of signing correctly Not backing up the ESP contents"},"baremetal/cloud-init-and-first-boot-configuration":{"html":"\u003cp>Building a new image for every server does not scale. Instead you build one image and let cloud-init configure each machine the first time it boots, using data supplied by the platform, a metadata service or a small seed disk.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Datasources, metadata and user data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing a cloud-config file\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The stages cloud-init runs through\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging a first boot that did not apply\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cloud-config\">cloud-config\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#cloud-config\nhostname: web-014\nusers:\n  - name: ops\n    groups: [sudo]\n    shell: /bin/bash\n    ssh_authorized_keys:\n      - ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nza... ops@example.com\npackage_update: true\npackages:\n  - chrony\n  - nginx\nwrite_files:\n  - path: /etc/motd\n    content: |\n      Managed by platform team\nruncmd:\n  - [systemctl, enable, --now, chrony]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">#cloud-config\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">hostname:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web-014\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">users:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ops\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">groups:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sudo\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">shell:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/bin/bash\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ssh_authorized_keys:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ssh-ed25519\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">AAAAC3Nza...\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ops@example.com\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">package_update:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">packages:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">chrony\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">nginx\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">write_files:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/etc/motd\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">content:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">|\n      Managed by platform team\n\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runcmd:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">systemctl\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">enable\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--now\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">chrony\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The file starts with \u003ccode>#cloud-config\u003c/code> and uses modules such as users, packages, write_files and runcmd. It is handed to the machine as \u003cstrong>user data\u003c/strong>, while \u003cstrong>metadata\u003c/strong> such as instance ID and hostname comes from the datasource: a cloud metadata service, a config drive or a NoCloud seed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stages\">Stages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>cloud-init runs in order: \u003cstrong>local\u003c/strong> (find the datasource), \u003cstrong>network\u003c/strong> (get metadata and user data), \u003cstrong>config\u003c/strong> (run configuration modules) and \u003cstrong>final\u003c/strong> (package installs and scripts). Most per-instance work runs only once, keyed by instance ID, so rebooting does not repeat it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"debugging\">Debugging\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cloud-init status --long\ncloud-init schema --system\njournalctl -u cloud-init -b\nless /var/log/cloud-init.log\nless /var/log/cloud-init-output.log\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">cloud-init status --long\ncloud-init schema --system\njournalctl -u cloud-init -b\nless /var/log/cloud-init.log\nless /var/log/cloud-init-output.log\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Check that the datasource was found, that the YAML is valid and which module failed. To re-test on a throwaway machine, run \u003ccode>cloud-init clean --logs\u003c/code> and reboot. Never do that on a node that already serves traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-node-with-no-keys\">Incident: The node with no keys\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A fresh server booted but nobody could log in.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cloud-init[611]: Failed to fetch metadata: connection timed out\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cloud-init[611]: no datasource found\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">status: error\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The network interface took longer than usual to come up, so cloud-init timed out and fell back to defaults without the SSH key. The team added a retry setting for the datasource, a post-boot health check that fails the node if cloud-init status is not done, and a rescue procedure through the BMC.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Putting secrets in user data that others can read\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming cloud-init runs every boot\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not validating the YAML schema\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Baking per-server details into the image\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"cloud-config","text":"cloud-config"},{"id":"stages","text":"Stages"},{"id":"debugging","text":"Debugging"},{"id":"incident-the-node-with-no-keys","text":"Incident: The node with no keys"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"cloud-init documentation","u":"https://cloudinit.readthedocs.io/en/latest/"},{"t":"Ubuntu server provisioning","u":"https://ubuntu.com/server/docs"}],"text":"Building a new image for every server does not scale. Instead you build one image and let cloud-init configure each machine the first time it boots, using data supplied by the platform, a metadata service or a small seed disk. What you will learn Datasources, metadata and user data Writing a cloud-config file The stages cloud-init runs through Debugging a first boot that did not apply cloud-config #cloud-config hostname: web-014 users: - name: ops groups: [ sudo ] shell: /bin/bash ssh_authorized_keys: - ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nza... ops@example.com package_update: true packages: - chrony - nginx write_files: - path: /etc/motd content: | Managed by platform team runcmd: - [ systemctl , enable , --now , chrony ] The file starts with #cloud-config and uses modules such as users, packages, write_files and runcmd. It is handed to the machine as user data , while metadata such as instance ID and hostname comes from the datasource: a cloud metadata service, a config drive or a NoCloud seed. Stages cloud-init runs in order: local (find the datasource), network (get metadata and user data), config (run configuration modules) and final (package installs and scripts). Most per-instance work runs only once, keyed by instance ID, so rebooting does not repeat it. Debugging cloud-init status --long cloud-init schema --system journalctl -u cloud-init -b less /var/log/cloud-init.log less /var/log/cloud-init-output.log Check that the datasource was found, that the YAML is valid and which module failed. To re-test on a throwaway machine, run cloud-init clean --logs and reboot. Never do that on a node that already serves traffic. Incident: The node with no keys Incident A fresh server booted but nobody could log in. cloud-init[611]: Failed to fetch metadata: connection timed out cloud-init[611]: no datasource found status: error The network interface took longer than usual to come up, so cloud-init timed out and fell back to defaults without the SSH key. The team added a retry setting for the datasource, a post-boot health check that fails the node if cloud-init status is not done, and a rescue procedure through the BMC. Common mistakes Putting secrets in user data that others can read Assuming cloud-init runs every boot Not validating the YAML schema Baking per-server details into the image"},"baremetal/firmware-and-bios-management-at-scale":{"html":"\u003cp>Two identical servers can behave differently because of a firmware version or a single BIOS option. At fleet scale you need a baseline, a safe update process and a way to notice drift before users do.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What firmware lives in a server\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Defining baselines for versions and BIOS settings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rolling updates in waves with rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Detecting and fixing drift\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-manage\">What to manage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>BIOS or UEFI settings:\u003c/strong> power profile, hyperthreading, virtualization, boot mode\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>BMC firmware:\u003c/strong> the management controller itself\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>NIC, HBA and RAID firmware:\u003c/strong> often the cause of odd packet loss or disk resets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Drive firmware:\u003c/strong> sometimes fixes known bugs or data-loss issues\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"baseline-and-inventory\">Baseline and inventory\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# baseline for one hardware model\nmodel: dl-gen11\nbios:\n  version: &quot;2.14.1&quot;\n  settings:\n    PowerProfile: MaxPerformance\n    Virtualization: Enabled\n    BootMode: Uefi\nbmc: &quot;3.20&quot;\nnic:\n  version: &quot;22.31.6&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># baseline for one hardware model\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">model:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">dl-gen11\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">bios:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2.14.1&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">settings:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">PowerProfile:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">MaxPerformance\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Virtualization:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Enabled\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">BootMode:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Uefi\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">bmc:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;3.20&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">nic:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;22.31.6&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read current values through Redfish, for example the \u003ccode>Bios\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>UpdateService\u003c/code> resources, or the vendor tool, and compare to the baseline. Store results with the server&#39;s asset record.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -sk -u &quot;admin:$BMC_PASS&quot; https://10.20.0.15/redfish/v1/Systems/1/Bios | jq '.Attributes.PowerProfile'\ncurl -sk -u &quot;admin:$BMC_PASS&quot; https://10.20.0.15/redfish/v1/UpdateService/FirmwareInventory | jq '.Members|length'\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">curl -sk -u \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;admin:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$BMC_PASS\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> https://10.20.0.15/redfish/v1/Systems/1/Bios | jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.Attributes.PowerProfile&#x27;\u003c/span>\ncurl -sk -u \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;admin:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$BMC_PASS\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> https://10.20.0.15/redfish/v1/UpdateService/FirmwareInventory | jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.Members|length&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rolling-out\">Rolling out\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Update one \u003cstrong>canary\u003c/strong> node per model, run burn-in and workload tests, then expand in small waves while watching error rates. Never update both members of a redundant pair at the same time, and drain workloads first. Keep the previous firmware image available, because some updates cannot be reverted and a rollback plan may mean replacing a part.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"drift\">Drift\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run a scheduled job that compares live values with the baseline and opens a ticket on mismatch. After repairs or part swaps, re-apply the baseline before the node rejoins the pool.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-slow-rack\">Incident: The slow rack\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A group of nodes ran 30 percent slower after maintenance.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"lscpu | grep MHz\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>lscpu | grep MHz\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CPU MHz:  1196.000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">BIOS PowerProfile: PowerSaving (baseline: MaxPerformance)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A mainboard swap reset the BIOS to defaults and nobody re-applied the baseline. CPUs sat at a low clock, and latency rose. The team added a post-repair step that applies and verifies the baseline and a weekly drift report across the fleet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Updating all nodes of a redundant pair together\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping BIOS settings in the baseline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not keeping old firmware images\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failing to re-apply the baseline after repairs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-to-manage","text":"What to manage"},{"id":"baseline-and-inventory","text":"Baseline and inventory"},{"id":"rolling-out","text":"Rolling out"},{"id":"drift","text":"Drift"},{"id":"incident-the-slow-rack","text":"Incident: The slow rack"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"DMTF Redfish standard","u":"https://www.dmtf.org/standards/redfish"},{"t":"Open Compute Project","u":"https://www.opencomputeproject.org/"}],"text":"Two identical servers can behave differently because of a firmware version or a single BIOS option. At fleet scale you need a baseline, a safe update process and a way to notice drift before users do. What you will learn What firmware lives in a server Defining baselines for versions and BIOS settings Rolling updates in waves with rollback Detecting and fixing drift What to manage BIOS or UEFI settings: power profile, hyperthreading, virtualization, boot mode BMC firmware: the management controller itself NIC, HBA and RAID firmware: often the cause of odd packet loss or disk resets Drive firmware: sometimes fixes known bugs or data-loss issues Baseline and inventory # baseline for one hardware model model: dl-gen11 bios: version: \"2.14.1\" settings: PowerProfile: MaxPerformance Virtualization: Enabled BootMode: Uefi bmc: \"3.20\" nic: version: \"22.31.6\" Read current values through Redfish, for example the Bios and UpdateService resources, or the vendor tool, and compare to the baseline. Store results with the server&#39;s asset record. curl -sk -u \"admin: $BMC_PASS \" https://10.20.0.15/redfish/v1/Systems/1/Bios | jq &#x27;.Attributes.PowerProfile&#x27; curl -sk -u \"admin: $BMC_PASS \" https://10.20.0.15/redfish/v1/UpdateService/FirmwareInventory | jq &#x27;.Members|length&#x27; Rolling out Update one canary node per model, run burn-in and workload tests, then expand in small waves while watching error rates. Never update both members of a redundant pair at the same time, and drain workloads first. Keep the previous firmware image available, because some updates cannot be reverted and a rollback plan may mean replacing a part. Drift Run a scheduled job that compares live values with the baseline and opens a ticket on mismatch. After repairs or part swaps, re-apply the baseline before the node rejoins the pool. Incident: The slow rack Incident A group of nodes ran 30 percent slower after maintenance. $ lscpu | grep MHz CPU MHz: 1196.000 BIOS PowerProfile: PowerSaving (baseline: MaxPerformance) A mainboard swap reset the BIOS to defaults and nobody re-applied the baseline. CPUs sat at a low clock, and latency rose. The team added a post-repair step that applies and verifies the baseline and a weekly drift report across the fleet. Common mistakes Updating all nodes of a redundant pair together Skipping BIOS settings in the baseline Not keeping old firmware images Failing to re-apply the baseline after repairs"},"baremetal/maas-and-ironic-bare-metal-provisioning":{"html":"\u003cp>Racking a server is not the end of the work. You must register it, test it, install an operating system and later wipe it. MAAS and Ironic automate that lifecycle so bare metal can be requested like a virtual machine.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The lifecycle: enroll, commission, allocate, deploy, release\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What commissioning and testing collect\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How deployment uses PXE, images and cloud-init\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing between MAAS and Ironic\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lifecycle\">Lifecycle\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Enroll:\u003c/strong> the machine PXE boots or is added with its BMC details.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Commission:\u003c/strong> a small ephemeral OS boots, inventories CPU, memory, disks and NICs and runs tests.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Ready and allocate:\u003c/strong> the server joins a pool and is reserved by a user or system.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Deploy:\u003c/strong> an image is written, networking is configured and cloud-init applies user data.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Release:\u003c/strong> the machine is wiped and returned to the pool.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"maas admin machines read | jq '.[]|{hostname,status_name,power_state}'\nmaas admin machines allocate cpu_count=32 tags=gpu\nmaas admin machine deploy &quot;$SYSTEM_ID&quot; distro_series=jammy user_data=&quot;$(base64 -w0 cloud-init.yaml)&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">maas admin machines \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">read\u003c/span> | jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.[]|{hostname,status_name,power_state}&#x27;\u003c/span>\nmaas admin machines allocate cpu_count=32 tags=gpu\nmaas admin machine deploy \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$SYSTEM_ID\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> distro_series=jammy user_data=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$(base64 -w0 cloud-init.yaml)\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ironic\">Ironic\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Ironic\u003c/strong> is the OpenStack bare metal service. It exposes servers as Nova flavors, so users boot a physical machine with the same API as a virtual one. It supports drivers for IPMI and Redfish, deploys over iPXE or virtual media and can perform automated cleaning between tenants.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"openstack baremetal node list\nopenstack server create --flavor baremetal-large --image jammy --network prod bm-web-01\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">openstack baremetal node list\nopenstack server create --flavor baremetal-large --image jammy --network prod bm-web-01\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Choose \u003cstrong>MAAS\u003c/strong> when you want a focused tool for datacenter provisioning with a simple web UI and API. Choose \u003cstrong>Ironic\u003c/strong> when you already run OpenStack and want bare metal and virtual machines under one control plane. Both rely on a reliable network boot setup and correct BMC credentials.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-server-that-kept-the-last-tenant-s-data\">Incident: The server that kept the last tenant&#39;s data\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A released machine was reallocated without cleaning.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"lsblk\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>lsblk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sda  1.8T  part /data/customer-a\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">released node 17 reused for tenant B without clean\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Cleaning was disabled to make release faster. The next tenant could read old data from the disk. The team enabled automated cleaning, verified it with a check after release and added a policy that a node may not return to the pool until wipe and firmware checks pass.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Skipping cleaning between users\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not testing hardware during commissioning\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Weak BMC credentials in the controller\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Relying on manual DHCP changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"lifecycle","text":"Lifecycle"},{"id":"ironic","text":"Ironic"},{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},{"id":"incident-the-server-that-kept-the-last-tenant-s-data","text":"Incident: The server that kept the last tenant's data"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"MAAS documentation","u":"https://maas.io/docs"},{"t":"OpenStack Ironic documentation","u":"https://docs.openstack.org/ironic/latest/"}],"text":"Racking a server is not the end of the work. You must register it, test it, install an operating system and later wipe it. MAAS and Ironic automate that lifecycle so bare metal can be requested like a virtual machine. What you will learn The lifecycle: enroll, commission, allocate, deploy, release What commissioning and testing collect How deployment uses PXE, images and cloud-init Choosing between MAAS and Ironic Lifecycle Enroll: the machine PXE boots or is added with its BMC details. Commission: a small ephemeral OS boots, inventories CPU, memory, disks and NICs and runs tests. Ready and allocate: the server joins a pool and is reserved by a user or system. Deploy: an image is written, networking is configured and cloud-init applies user data. Release: the machine is wiped and returned to the pool. maas admin machines read | jq &#x27;.[]|{hostname,status_name,power_state}&#x27; maas admin machines allocate cpu_count=32 tags=gpu maas admin machine deploy \" $SYSTEM_ID \" distro_series=jammy user_data= \" $(base64 -w0 cloud-init.yaml) \" Ironic Ironic is the OpenStack bare metal service. It exposes servers as Nova flavors, so users boot a physical machine with the same API as a virtual one. It supports drivers for IPMI and Redfish, deploys over iPXE or virtual media and can perform automated cleaning between tenants. openstack baremetal node list openstack server create --flavor baremetal-large --image jammy --network prod bm-web-01 Choosing Choose MAAS when you want a focused tool for datacenter provisioning with a simple web UI and API. Choose Ironic when you already run OpenStack and want bare metal and virtual machines under one control plane. Both rely on a reliable network boot setup and correct BMC credentials. Incident: The server that kept the last tenant&#39;s data Incident A released machine was reallocated without cleaning. $ lsblk sda 1.8T part /data/customer-a released node 17 reused for tenant B without clean Cleaning was disabled to make release faster. The next tenant could read old data from the disk. The team enabled automated cleaning, verified it with a check after release and added a policy that a node may not return to the pool until wipe and firmware checks pass. Common mistakes Skipping cleaning between users Not testing hardware during commissioning Weak BMC credentials in the controller Relying on manual DHCP changes"},"baremetal/hardware-failure-diagnosis-and-rma":{"html":"\u003cp>Most hardware failures leave clues before the outage. The skill is knowing where to look, which counters matter and how to hand the vendor enough proof to approve a replacement quickly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading the system event log, ECC counters and SMART data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Telling memory, disk, CPU and power problems apart\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Collecting evidence for an RMA\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Verifying and recording the repair\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-the-clues-are\">Where the clues are\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ipmitool sel elist | tail -5\nedac-util -v\ndmesg -T | grep -iE &quot;mce|edac|machine check|i/o error&quot;\nsmartctl -a /dev/sda | grep -E &quot;Reallocated|Pending|Uncorrectable|Power_On&quot;\nsensors\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ipmitool sel elist | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -5\nedac-util -v\ndmesg -T | grep -iE \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;mce|edac|machine check|i/o error&quot;\u003c/span>\nsmartctl -a /dev/sda | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Reallocated|Pending|Uncorrectable|Power_On&quot;\u003c/span>\nsensors\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Memory:\u003c/strong> corrected ECC errors that keep rising on one DIMM, then uncorrected errors and machine check events\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Disks:\u003c/strong> reallocated or pending sectors, I/O errors, long command timeouts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>CPU and board:\u003c/strong> machine check exceptions, thermal throttling, random reboots\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Power:\u003c/strong> PSU failure events, voltage warnings, losing redundancy\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"diagnosis-method\">Diagnosis method\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Drain the workload first. Read the SEL for the first error, not just the last, and note the slot or serial. Run the vendor&#39;s diagnostic or a memory test to reproduce the fault, and swap the suspected part only after the evidence points at it. Changing many parts at once hides the cause.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rma\">RMA\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Vendors want the serial number, the diagnostic output, the SEL and the exact error. Attach them to one ticket, request an advance replacement when downtime matters and label the failed part for return. After the swap, run burn-in, re-apply the firmware and BIOS baseline, and record the part serial against the server.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"smartctl -x /dev/sda &gt; /var/tmp/sda-smart.txt\nipmitool sel list &gt; /var/tmp/sel.txt\ndmidecode -t memory | grep -E &quot;Locator|Serial|Size&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">smartctl -x /dev/sda &gt; /var/tmp/sda-smart.txt\nipmitool sel list &gt; /var/tmp/sel.txt\ndmidecode -t memory | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Locator|Serial|Size&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-weekly-crash\">Incident: The weekly crash\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A server crashed once a week until one DIMM was replaced.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">EDAC MC0: 1 CE memory read error on CPU_SrcID#0_Channel#2_DIMM#1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">EDAC MC0: 14 CE memory read errors on the same DIMM\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">mce: [Hardware Error]: uncorrected memory error\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Nobody watched corrected error counts. They grew for weeks and then became an uncorrectable fault that crashed the kernel. After the swap the team added an alert on corrected errors per DIMM and a policy to replace modules before uncorrected errors appear.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Replacing several parts at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring corrected errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sending the vendor vague descriptions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping burn-in after a repair\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"where-the-clues-are","text":"Where the clues are"},{"id":"diagnosis-method","text":"Diagnosis method"},{"id":"rma","text":"RMA"},{"id":"incident-the-weekly-crash","text":"Incident: The weekly crash"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"smartmontools","u":"https://www.smartmontools.org/"},{"t":"Linux kernel documentation","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/"}],"text":"Most hardware failures leave clues before the outage. The skill is knowing where to look, which counters matter and how to hand the vendor enough proof to approve a replacement quickly. What you will learn Reading the system event log, ECC counters and SMART data Telling memory, disk, CPU and power problems apart Collecting evidence for an RMA Verifying and recording the repair Where the clues are ipmitool sel elist | tail -5 edac-util -v dmesg -T | grep -iE \"mce|edac|machine check|i/o error\" smartctl -a /dev/sda | grep -E \"Reallocated|Pending|Uncorrectable|Power_On\" sensors Memory: corrected ECC errors that keep rising on one DIMM, then uncorrected errors and machine check events Disks: reallocated or pending sectors, I/O errors, long command timeouts CPU and board: machine check exceptions, thermal throttling, random reboots Power: PSU failure events, voltage warnings, losing redundancy Diagnosis method Drain the workload first. Read the SEL for the first error, not just the last, and note the slot or serial. Run the vendor&#39;s diagnostic or a memory test to reproduce the fault, and swap the suspected part only after the evidence points at it. Changing many parts at once hides the cause. RMA Vendors want the serial number, the diagnostic output, the SEL and the exact error. Attach them to one ticket, request an advance replacement when downtime matters and label the failed part for return. After the swap, run burn-in, re-apply the firmware and BIOS baseline, and record the part serial against the server. smartctl -x /dev/sda > /var/tmp/sda-smart.txt ipmitool sel list > /var/tmp/sel.txt dmidecode -t memory | grep -E \"Locator|Serial|Size\" Incident: The weekly crash Incident A server crashed once a week until one DIMM was replaced. EDAC MC0: 1 CE memory read error on CPU_SrcID#0_Channel#2_DIMM#1 EDAC MC0: 14 CE memory read errors on the same DIMM mce: [Hardware Error]: uncorrected memory error Nobody watched corrected error counts. They grew for weeks and then became an uncorrectable fault that crashed the kernel. After the swap the team added an alert on corrected errors per DIMM and a policy to replace modules before uncorrected errors appear. Common mistakes Replacing several parts at once Ignoring corrected errors Sending the vendor vague descriptions Skipping burn-in after a repair"},"baremetal/power-cooling-and-rack-capacity":{"html":"\u003cp>You can fill a rack with servers and still be unable to power or cool them. Good operators plan in kilowatts and airflow, keep redundancy honest and watch the real numbers from the power distribution units.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading watts, amps and power factor for a rack\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Redundant A and B feeds and the 80 percent rule\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Airflow, inlet temperature and hot spots\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tracking stranded and available capacity\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"power-budget\">Power budget\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Estimate from \u003cstrong>measured\u003c/strong> draw at typical and peak load, not from the label on the power supply. Convert: watts divided by voltage gives amps, so a 7 kW rack on 230 V draws about 30 A. Breakers should not run continuously above about 80 percent of their rating.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# read power from a server BMC\nipmitool dcmi power reading\n# many PDUs expose SNMP or Redfish; example SNMP read\nsnmpget -v2c -c public pdu-a-12 .1.3.6.1.4.1.318.1.1.12.1.16.0\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># read power from a server BMC\u003c/span>\nipmitool dcmi power reading\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># many PDUs expose SNMP or Redfish; example SNMP read\u003c/span>\nsnmpget -v2c -c public pdu-a-12 .1.3.6.1.4.1.318.1.1.12.1.16.0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"redundant-feeds\">Redundant feeds\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With \u003cstrong>A and B\u003c/strong> feeds, each server draws from both. If one feed fails, the other carries everything, so the \u003cstrong>sum of both\u003c/strong> must fit within one feed. That means total load must stay under roughly 40 percent of the combined rating, or under 80 percent of one feed. Balance servers across phases so no single phase overloads.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cooling\">Cooling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Servers pull cold air in at the front and exhaust hot air at the back. Keep cold and hot aisles separate, use blanking panels in empty slots and watch \u003cstrong>inlet temperature\u003c/strong> sensors on servers. High inlet temperature means recirculation or too much heat in one place. Dense GPU racks may need liquid cooling or fewer servers per rack.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity-tracking\">Capacity tracking\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Record per rack: rated power, measured peak, space left, cooling limit and network ports. The smallest of these is the real capacity. Unused space with no spare power is \u003cstrong>stranded\u003c/strong>. Review monthly and before every order.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-one-feed-fails-the-other-trips\">Incident: One feed fails, the other trips\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Losing feed A overloaded feed B and a breaker opened.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">PDU-A: input lost\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">PDU-B: load 31.4A of 32A rated\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">PDU-B: breaker tripped, 24 servers down\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The rack had been designed as redundant, but both feeds already ran near capacity. When A failed, B took the full load and tripped. After the outage the team set a rule that the combined load must fit on one feed with headroom, and added alerts at 70 percent per feed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Budgeting from nameplate instead of measured draw\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting A and B feeds both run near their limit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving gaps in racks without blanking panels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring inlet temperature trends\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"power-budget","text":"Power budget"},{"id":"redundant-feeds","text":"Redundant feeds"},{"id":"cooling","text":"Cooling"},{"id":"capacity-tracking","text":"Capacity tracking"},{"id":"incident-one-feed-fails-the-other-trips","text":"Incident: One feed fails, the other trips"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Open Compute Project","u":"https://www.opencomputeproject.org/"},{"t":"Supermicro resources","u":"https://www.supermicro.com/"}],"text":"You can fill a rack with servers and still be unable to power or cool them. Good operators plan in kilowatts and airflow, keep redundancy honest and watch the real numbers from the power distribution units. What you will learn Reading watts, amps and power factor for a rack Redundant A and B feeds and the 80 percent rule Airflow, inlet temperature and hot spots Tracking stranded and available capacity Power budget Estimate from measured draw at typical and peak load, not from the label on the power supply. Convert: watts divided by voltage gives amps, so a 7 kW rack on 230 V draws about 30 A. Breakers should not run continuously above about 80 percent of their rating. # read power from a server BMC ipmitool dcmi power reading # many PDUs expose SNMP or Redfish; example SNMP read snmpget -v2c -c public pdu-a-12 .1.3.6.1.4.1.318.1.1.12.1.16.0 Redundant feeds With A and B feeds, each server draws from both. If one feed fails, the other carries everything, so the sum of both must fit within one feed. That means total load must stay under roughly 40 percent of the combined rating, or under 80 percent of one feed. Balance servers across phases so no single phase overloads. Cooling Servers pull cold air in at the front and exhaust hot air at the back. Keep cold and hot aisles separate, use blanking panels in empty slots and watch inlet temperature sensors on servers. High inlet temperature means recirculation or too much heat in one place. Dense GPU racks may need liquid cooling or fewer servers per rack. Capacity tracking Record per rack: rated power, measured peak, space left, cooling limit and network ports. The smallest of these is the real capacity. Unused space with no spare power is stranded . Review monthly and before every order. Incident: One feed fails, the other trips Incident Losing feed A overloaded feed B and a breaker opened. PDU-A: input lost PDU-B: load 31.4A of 32A rated PDU-B: breaker tripped, 24 servers down The rack had been designed as redundant, but both feeds already ran near capacity. When A failed, B took the full load and tripped. After the outage the team set a rule that the combined load must fit on one feed with headroom, and added alerts at 70 percent per feed. Common mistakes Budgeting from nameplate instead of measured draw Letting A and B feeds both run near their limit Leaving gaps in racks without blanking panels Ignoring inlet temperature trends"}});